l–quantum networks introduce new forensic challenges due to quantum state collapse, decoherence and the no cloning theorem. These features invalidate traditional evidence preservation assumptions. Existing digital forensic frameworks lack proactive mechanisms which are capable of ensuring integrity and readiness in such hybrid environments. This paper proposes the Quantum Forensic Readiness Architecture (QFRA) which is mainly a distributed framework. The framework consists of four major components. They are (i) Quantum Evidence Objects (QEOs) for structured quantum metadata representation, (ii) Forensic Entanglement Mapping (FEM) for hybrid event correlation, (iii) a Decoherence Aware Replication Protocol (QEPP) for adaptive evidence preservation and (iv) a post quantum cryptography based distributed ledger for tamper evident storage. QFRA is implemented in a NetSquid based hybrid sim

